== 2...g6 · King's Indian defence ==
With 2...g6, Black intends to fianchetto their dark square bishop with ...Bg7. Because ...g6 weakens the f6 and h6 dark squares on the king's side, the bishop is important to defend the king after Black castles. In addition, from g7 the bishop will exert pressure on the long diagonal and target White's centre. 

[[/3. Nc3|'''3. Nc3''']] is the main move. This prepares to play e4. Usually, White employs a set-up with Be2, Nf3 and O-O and  will attack on the queen's side. Black continues with 3...Bg7. Black usually attacks on the king's side, and looks for pawn breaks with ...e5 and...f5, or sometimes ...c5 and ...b5. The main side line is 3...d5, the Grünfeld defence, the main line of which involves trading the knights: 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 Nxc3 6. bxc3. 

White has a few sidelines, though sometimes these are just changes in move order. One decision that makes a difference is where White would like to play their king's bishop. In the main line it develops to e2, but they may also choose to fianchetto it, in which case they may start with [[/3. g3|'''3. g3''']] or [[/3. Nf3|'''3. Nf3''']] Bg7 4. g3, the fianchetto variation. In these positions, White may avoid playing e4, which would inhibit their bishop's vision on the long diagonal. 

Also to consider are the anti-Grünfeld lines. [[/3. f3|'''3. f3''']], the Alekhine variation, supports playing e4 with a pawn instead of the knight. The idea is that if 3...d5 anyway, 4. cxd5 Nxd5 5. e4 and Black can't trade the knights and must play 5...Nb6. [[/3. d5|'''3. d5''']], the advance variation, the idea being Black cannot play 3...d5 if White's pawn is already there. 

Finally, [[/3. h4|'''3. h4''']] is the '''Basman-Williams''' attack. 

=== History ===
The Indian defences are named for their use by Indian chess masters in the 19th century, and were introduced to Europe especially though the games of [[w:John Cochrane (chess master)|John Cochrane]] against Kolkata chess masters [[w:Moheschunder Bannerjee|Moheschunder Bannerjee]] and Saumchurn Guttack. 

The king's Indian defence is the archetypal [[w:hypermodernism (chess)|hypermodern]] opening and received renewed interest in the 1920s. The hypermodernists thought to control the centre with pieces at range, as opposed to the classical school of though which was to occupy the centre with one's pawns. 

In the 20th century it grew in popularity and was favoured by many of the strongest players, including by World Champions [[w:Garry Kasparov|Garry Kasparov]]<ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chess.pl?page=2&pid=15940&playercomp=black&eco=E60-E99 Garry Kasparov's games in the kings's Indian as Black, Chessgames.com]</ref> and [[w:Bobby Fischer|Bobby Fischer]].<ref>[https://www.chessgames.com/perl/chess.pl?page=5&pid=19233&playercomp=black&eco=E60-E99 Bobby Fischer's games in the king's Indian as Black, Chessgames.com]</ref> Today it is seen as one of the most reputable ways for Black to meet d4 openings. 

Tartakower defined 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 g6 as the ''alt-Indisch'' (German: '''old Indian''') (with ...d6 being played later. The "New" Indian was with 2...e6 and later ...b6).<ref>{{Cite book |title=Die hypermoderne Schachpartie |last=Tartakower |first=S. G. |publisher=Wiener Schaczeitung |year=1924 |location=Vienna |pages=411-425 |language=German |url=https://archive.org/details/diehypermodernes00tart_0}}</ref> Nowadays "old Indian" usually refers to a set-up with 2...d6, playing for ...e5 without the idea of fianchettoing the king's bishop: 1. d4 Nf6 2. c4 d6 3. Nc3 Nbd7 4. e4 e5 5. Nf3 Be7. Lichess's opening book refers to this position as the '''West Indian''', reserving the "king's" soubriquet until after White has moved, though this is atypical.

